## IP Addressing Scheme

| Link Category | Subnet Pattern | Size | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| LAN segments | `10.10.S.0/24` | /24 | Switch + PCs at each site |
| Primary chain links | `10.20.N.0/30` | /30 | HQ → LHR → KHI → ISB → PEW → MUL |
| Internal links | `10.30.S.0/30` | /30 | EDGE1 ↔ EDGE2 at each site |
| Backup / core mesh | `10.40.N.0/30` | /30 | EDGE2 ↔ CORE-T, and CORE-T ring |
| GRE tunnels | `10.50.N.0/30` | /30 | HQ-EDGE2 ↔ each branch EDGE2 |
| Loopbacks | `10.99.0.R/32` | /32 | OSPF router-id, tunnel source/destination |

---

## Verification Summary

- ✅ All WAN links show `Encapsulation PPP, LCP Open` — PPP/CHAP authentication successful
- ✅ OSPF neighbors in `FULL` state across the primary chain, internal links, and GRE tunnels
- ✅ GRE tunnels `up/up` between HQ-EDGE2 and branch EDGE2 routers
- ✅ Floating static routes present (`S* 0.0.0.0/0 [150/0]`) on every branch EDGE1
- ✅ NAT/PAT configuration active on HQ-EDGE1 (inside/outside interfaces, overload)
- ✅ End-to-end ping and traceroute successful between branch PCs and HQ across multiple test paths

---

## Key Design Decisions

- **Chain topology instead of a star** for primary links, to distribute port
  load across routers rather than concentrating it on HQ.
- **Separate EDGE1/EDGE2 routers per site**, connected by an internal link,
  so that a primary-link failure on EDGE1 can hand traffic to EDGE2's
  independent backup path without any change at the end-user PCs.
- **Single-area OSPF**, appropriate for a network of this size — multi-area
  hierarchy would add configuration overhead without benefit at 15 routers.

